Abstract
A system and method for a self-managing, quantum-secured sixth-generation (6G)
communications network is disclosed. The system comprises a tri-layered architecture
featuring a 6G Transport Layer for high-throughput data transmission, a Quantum Security
Layer (QSL) for providing information-theoretic security, and a Neuromorphic Cognitive
Orchestration Layer (NCOL) for autonomous network management. The QSL utilizes a hybrid
of Post-Quantum Cryptography (PQC) and Quantum Key Distribution (QKD) to secure
network communications against both classical and quantum threats. The NCOL,
implemented via a distributed network of neuromorphic co-processors running Spiking Neural
Networks (SNNs), provides real-time, low-latency, and energy-efficient control over both the
transport and security layers. Key inventive steps include a method for neuromorphic
management of QKD trusted relays, wherein the NCOL dynamically assesses relay
trustworthiness to route quantum keys along the most secure paths, thereby mitigating
critical vulnerabilities in prior art. The invention further includes a system for emergent
intent-based networking, where the network self-organizes to fulfill high-level service
objectives, and a method for predictive self-healing based on microsecond-level anomaly
detection. A quantum-state-aware digital twin, managed by the NCOL, enables high-fidelity
simulation and pre-validation of network optimizations, including the probabilistic behavior of
the quantum layer, facilitating true network self-evolution.
Background of the Invention
Field of the Invention
This invention pertains to the field of telecommunications, specifically to the architecture,
management, and security of next-generation wireless networks. It further relates to the
integration of quantum communication protocols and neuromorphic computing systems for
autonomous network orchestration, providing a resilient, intelligent, and secure infrastructure
for future digital services.
Description of Related Art
The evolution of wireless communication has been characterized by generational leaps in
capability, each enabling new paradigms of connectivity and application. The fifth generation
(5G) of wireless technology delivered significant advancements through three primary service
categories: enhanced Mobile Broadband (eMBB), Ultra-Reliable Low-Latency
Communications (URLLC), and massive Machine-Type Communications (mMTC).
1 These
capabilities have begun to power the Internet of Things (IoT), connected vehicles, and
immersive media. However, the architectural underpinnings of 5G networks, while a
substantial improvement over previous generations, face fundamental limitations in scalability,
energy consumption, and management complexity as the world moves towards a true Internet
of Everything (IoE), where trillions of devices are expected to be connected.
2
Projections for the sixth generation (6G) of wireless technology, expected to be
commercialized around 2030, envision a network that is not merely an incremental
improvement but a transformative platform.
4 Key performance indicators for 6G include peak
data rates of up to 1 terabit per second (Tbps), user-experienced data rates of 1 gigabit per
second (Gbps), air latency below 100 microseconds, and connection densities exceeding 10
million devices per square kilometer.
2 Achieving this vision requires the adoption of new
technologies such as the use of Terahertz (THz) frequency bands, cell-free massive
Multiple-Input Multiple-Output (MIMO) antenna systems, and Integrated Sensing and
Communication (ISAC), where the network infrastructure simultaneously communicates and
senses the physical environment.
6 This leap in performance and functionality will create a
network of unprecedented complexity, where traditional network management and
orchestration (MANO) systems, even those augmented with conventional Artificial Intelligence
(AI), will be insufficient.
9 The 6G network is envisioned as an AI-native system, a "distributed
neural network" that fuses the physical, biological, and cyber worlds.
11 This inherent
complexity demands a new paradigm of autonomous, intelligent, and efficient control.
Concurrently with the development of 6G, the field of quantum computing is rapidly
advancing, posing a catastrophic threat to the security infrastructure that underpins the
entire digital economy. Current public-key cryptography systems, such as RSA and Elliptic
Curve Cryptography (ECC), rely on the computational difficulty of mathematical problems like
factoring large integers.
12 A sufficiently powerful quantum computer, running algorithms like
Shor's algorithm, will be able to solve these problems in a trivial amount of time, rendering
nearly all currently deployed secure communication protocols obsolete.
13 This vulnerability is
not a distant threat but an immediate one, due to the "harvest now, decrypt later" attack
vector.
14 Adversaries are actively intercepting and storing vast quantities of encrypted data
today, with the intention of decrypting it once a cryptographically relevant quantum computer
becomes available. This compromises the long-term security of sensitive government,
financial, and personal data transmitted over current networks.
17
In response to this quantum threat, two primary security solutions have emerged:
Post-Quantum Cryptography (PQC) and Quantum Key Distribution (QKD). PQC involves the
development of new classical cryptographic algorithms based on mathematical problems
believed to be hard for both classical and quantum computers.
12 While PQC offers a
software-based upgrade path, its long-term security is based on unproven computational
assumptions. QKD, conversely, offers information-theoretic security based on the
fundamental laws of quantum mechanics, such as the no-cloning theorem and the principle
that measurement disturbs a quantum system.
20 QKD allows two parties to generate a shared
secret key with the guarantee that any attempt by an eavesdropper to intercept the key will be
detected. However, the practical deployment of QKD over wide-area networks is severely
hampered by a critical architectural flaw: its reliance on "trusted relays" or "trusted nodes".
19
Due to photon loss in optical fiber, QKD links are typically limited to distances of a few
hundred kilometers.
23 To extend this range, intermediate nodes must receive the quantum
signal, convert it to a classical key, and then retransmit it on a new quantum link. These
trusted relays possess the full secret key and thus represent highly attractive targets for
physical or cyber-attack. The compromise of a single trusted relay can invalidate the security
of the entire communication chain, a vulnerability that significantly curtails the promise of
QKD.
19
To manage the complexity of 5G and future 6G networks, AI and Machine Learning (ML) have
been widely adopted for tasks such as network optimization, traffic management, and
predictive maintenance.
6 Existing patents, including those held by the assignee, describe
systems for AI-driven network reconciliation and security threat detection.
27 However, these AI
systems are predominantly based on conventional von Neumann computing architectures,
such as Graphics Processing Units (GPUs) and Tensor Processing Units (TPUs). While
powerful for offline training of large models, these architectures are ill-suited for the
real-time, distributed, and energy-constrained demands of 6G network control. They exhibit
high energy consumption, introduce significant latency in the decision-making loop, and
operate on a reactive or slow predictive cycle based on large historical datasets.
30 This makes
them incapable of responding to network dynamics at the microsecond timescale required for
6G URLLC services or detecting subtle, real-time anomalies indicative of sophisticated
security breaches.
The prior art thus presents a set of profound, interdependent challenges. The escalating
complexity of 6G networks demands a more powerful form of autonomous management. The
looming quantum threat necessitates a new security paradigm, yet the most promising
solution, QKD, contains a fundamental vulnerability. The conventional AI tools used to manage
network complexity are too slow, power-hungry, and reactive to effectively control a 6G
system or secure a quantum network in real time. What is needed, and what is absent from
the prior art, is a unified architecture that holistically solves these interconnected
problems—a system that is simultaneously ultra-fast, ultra-secure, and ultra-intelligent by
design.
