Detailed Description of the Invention
The present invention provides a comprehensive architecture for a next-generation
communications network that is fundamentally autonomous, secure, and efficient. The system
is designed to meet and exceed the projected demands of the 6G era by synergistically
combining advances in wireless communication, quantum physics, and neuromorphic
computing.
I. System Architecture: The Tri-Layered Cognitive Network
Referring to FIG. 1, the system architecture 100 is logically and functionally partitioned into
three distinct yet deeply integrated layers: a 6G Transport Layer 110, a Quantum Security
Layer (QSL) 120, and a Neuromorphic Cognitive Orchestration Layer (NCOL) 130. This layered
approach allows for separation of concerns while enabling powerful cross-layer optimization
and control facilitated by the NCOL 130.
The 6G Transport Layer 110 constitutes the physical infrastructure responsible for data
transmission and sensing. This layer embodies the technological advancements anticipated
for 6G networks.
8
It comprises a Radio Access Network (RAN) featuring cell-free massive
MIMO antenna arrays and operating in frequency bands up to and including the Terahertz
(THz) spectrum to achieve multi-terabit-per-second data rates.
2 A core component of this
layer is Integrated Sensing and Communication (ISAC), wherein network elements such as
base stations and user equipment (UE) not only transmit and receive data but also actively
sense the physical environment by analyzing radio wave reflections.
7 This provides a rich,
real-time stream of contextual data about the network's operating environment. The transport
layer is further supported by a high-capacity optical fiber backhaul and a cloud-native,
service-based core network architecture that is disaggregated and virtualized, running on
commodity hardware from the network core to the far edge.
7 This layer serves as the conduit
for all user plane data, control plane signaling, and the raw sensory data ingested by the
NCOL 130.
The Quantum Security Layer (QSL) 120 is an overlay network dedicated to providing robust,
future-proof cryptographic services. It operates in parallel with the 6G Transport Layer 110,
generating and distributing secure keys that are used to encrypt traffic flowing through the
transport layer. The QSL 120 is implemented primarily over the assignee's existing and
future-deployed optical fiber infrastructure, which is a prerequisite for practical quantum
communication.
40 The QSL 120 is not a monolithic security solution but a hybrid framework,
as will be detailed in Section II. Its primary function is to secure the network against attacks
from both classical supercomputers and future fault-tolerant quantum computers, addressing
the critical "harvest now, decrypt later" threat.
14
The Neuromorphic Cognitive Orchestration Layer (NCOL) 130 is the intelligent control
plane and the central nervous system of the entire architecture. It is a distributed intelligence
system, physically realized by deploying specialized neuromorphic co-processors (NPUs) at
strategic points throughout the network infrastructure, including base stations, edge data
centers, and core network nodes.
42 These NPUs are designed to execute Spiking Neural
Networks (SNNs), a class of brain-inspired AI models that operate asynchronously and in an
event-driven manner.
34 This architecture allows the NCOL 130 to monitor the state of both the
6G Transport Layer 110 and the QSL 120 with microsecond-level latency and extremely low
power consumption.
30
It receives high-dimensional, real-time data streams—including traffic
statistics, ISAC environmental data, and quantum channel state information—and uses this
data to perform autonomous orchestration, predictive maintenance, and dynamic security
management, as detailed in subsequent sections. The NCOL 130 replaces the slow,
energy-intensive, and reactive control loops of conventional AI-managed networks with a
proactive, adaptive, and highly efficient cognitive engine.
II. The Quantum Security Layer (QSL)
The QSL 120 is designed to provide end-to-end, long-term security for all communications
within the network. It achieves this through a sophisticated hybrid framework that combines
the strengths of multiple quantum-resistant technologies, ensuring both immediate and
future protection.
Hybrid Security Framework
The security of the QSL 120 is founded on a two-pronged approach:
1. Post-Quantum Cryptography (PQC): The system utilizes PQC algorithms, such as
those standardized by the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST), for
critical functions like digital signatures, authentication, and initial key establishment.
14
These are classical algorithms designed to run on conventional hardware but are based
on mathematical problems (e.g., lattice-based, code-based, or hash-based problems)
that are believed to be intractable for both classical and quantum computers.
12 PQC
provides a crucial baseline of security, enabling a secure boot-strapping process for
network elements and securing control plane communications. It offers a practical,
software-based path to quantum readiness that can be deployed across the entire
network, including on resource-constrained IoT devices where implementing QKD is not
feasible.
44
2. Quantum Key Distribution (QKD): For securing high-value user plane data, particularly
for services demanding the highest level of long-term confidentiality, the QSL 120
employs QKD. As shown in FIG. 2, QKD protocols (e.g., BB84 or entanglement-based
variants) are used to establish symmetric cryptographic keys between two endpoints
(e.g., Alice 210 and Bob 220) over a dedicated quantum channel 230, typically an optical
fiber.
21 The security of these keys is not based on computational assumptions but on the
laws of quantum physics. Any attempt by an eavesdropper (Eve 240) to measure the
qubits in transit will inevitably disturb their state, introducing detectable errors and
alerting Alice and Bob to the presence of an attack.
21 This provides information-theoretic
security and forward secrecy, ensuring that keys established today cannot be
compromised by any future computational advancements, including quantum
computers.
20
Inventive Step 1: Neuromorphic-Managed Trusted Relay System
A fundamental challenge limiting the scalability and practical security of QKD is its reliance on
trusted relays 250 to extend its range beyond approximately 100-200 km.
23 Each trusted relay
250 is a node that terminates a QKD link, classically processes the key, and initiates a new
QKD link. This node becomes a point of absolute trust, as it holds the unencrypted key
material.
19 A physical or cyber compromise of a single relay can expose the entire key chain,
undermining the end-to-end security guarantee of QKD. This invention introduces a novel
system to transform this brittle, static trust model into a dynamic, resilient, and continuously
verified one, managed by the NCOL 130.
The process, illustrated in the flowchart of FIG. 4, replaces the binary concept of a "trusted
node" with a continuous, real-time "Trust Score."
1. Continuous Data Ingestion (Step 410): Each QKD relay 250 and next-generation
quantum repeater 260 in the network is equipped with sensors that feed data to a local
or nearby NCOL neuromorphic processor. This data includes classical operational
parameters (e.g., power consumption, temperature, CPU/memory utilization, network
traffic on classical channels) and quantum performance metrics (e.g., Quantum Bit Error
Rate (QBER), sifted key rate, photon detection counts, entanglement fidelity).
2. Neuromorphic Anomaly Detection (Step 420): A dedicated SNN within the NCOL is
trained to recognize the baseline, high-dimensional spatio-temporal signature of a
"healthy" relay. Unlike conventional systems that monitor individual thresholds, the SNN
learns the complex correlations between all parameters. Its event-driven nature allows it
to operate with microsecond latency, detecting subtle deviations that may signal a
sophisticated attack, such as a side-channel attack attempting to glean information from
power fluctuations or timing variations, or the initial stages of a physical tampering
event.
36 The system's ability to process sparse, event-driven data makes it exceptionally
efficient for this continuous monitoring task.
34
3. Dynamic Trust Scoring (Step 430): Based on the output of the anomaly detection SNN,
the NCOL assigns a continuously updated Trust Score, a value between 0 and 1, to each
relay node. A score of 1 represents a perfectly healthy node operating within all expected
parameters. The detection of any anomaly, even a minor one, causes an immediate decay
in the score, with the magnitude of the decay proportional to the severity of the anomaly.
The score gradually recovers back towards 1 during periods of sustained nominal
operation. This transforms trust from a static assumption into a dynamic, quantifiable
metric.
4. Intelligent Key Routing (Step 440): When an application requests a secure QKD
channel between two endpoints (e.g., Alice 210 and Bob 220), the request is handled by
the NCOL. The NCOL models the QKD network as a weighted graph, where the relays are
vertices and the links are edges. Instead of using physical distance or latency as the
primary path weight, it uses the inverse of the Trust Score. The NCOL then executes a
pathfinding algorithm (Step 450) to determine the route through the network that
maximizes the product of the Trust Scores of the relays along the path. This ensures that
the cryptographic key is always generated and relayed through the chain of nodes that
is, at that precise moment, demonstrably the most secure. If the trust score of a relay on
an active path drops below a critical threshold, the NCOL can proactively trigger a key
re-establishment on an alternative, more trusted path before a compromise can occur
(Step 460).
This neuromorphic management system effectively solves the Achilles' heel of QKD. It creates
a security infrastructure with biological-like reflexes, capable of identifying and isolating
threats at the speed of physical events, thereby enabling the deployment of a scalable and
genuinely secure quantum network. This approach also extends to future quantum repeaters
260, where the NCOL can manage the complex processes of entanglement swapping and
purification, optimizing performance based on real-time measurements of entanglement
fidelity.
50
III. The Neuromorphic Cognitive Orchestration Layer (NCOL)
The NCOL 130 represents a fundamental departure from traditional network control planes. It
is designed to emulate the efficiency, adaptability, and parallelism of the biological brain to
manage the immense complexity of the quantum-secured 6G network.
Hardware and Software Implementation
As shown in FIG. 3, the NCOL is not a centralized entity but a distributed intelligence fabric. It
is composed of a multitude of neuromorphic co-processors (NPUs) 310, such as Intel's Loihi 2
or a future proprietary AT&T design, integrated into network equipment at all levels, from the
edge (e.g., gNodeB 320) to the core (e.g., Core Network Functions 330).
43 These NPUs are not
von Neumann processors; they are purpose-built silicon architectures that physically
implement artificial neurons and synapses.
34
The software running on these NPUs consists of Spiking Neural Networks (SNNs) 340. In an
SNN, information is encoded in the timing of discrete events, or "spikes," much like the action
potentials in the brain.
35 Neurons integrate incoming spikes over time, and when their internal
membrane potential reaches a threshold, they fire a spike of their own. This event-driven,
asynchronous mode of computation has profound advantages over the clock-driven, dense
matrix multiplications of conventional Artificial Neural Networks (ANNs)
30
:
● Ultra-Low Power: Neurons and synapses only consume power when they are actively
processing a spike. For the sparse activity typical of many real-world monitoring tasks,
this results in orders-of-magnitude lower energy consumption compared to GPUs.
31
● Ultra-Low Latency: Processing occurs as soon as an event (a spike) arrives, eliminating
the frame-based processing delays of conventional systems. This enables
decision-making on microsecond timescales.
33
● Inherent Temporality: SNNs are naturally suited to processing complex, time-varying
data streams, such as network traffic patterns or quantum channel fluctuations, without
the need for complex recurrent architectures like LSTMs.
56
● Plasticity: SNNs can incorporate local learning rules, such as Spike-Timing-Dependent
Plasticity (STDP), where the strength of a synapse is modified based on the relative
timing of pre- and post-synaptic spikes. This allows the network to learn and adapt
continuously in real-time.
35
Inventive Step 2: Emergent Intent-Based Networking (IBN)
Current IBN frameworks, while a step towards automation, are fundamentally procedural. A
human operator expresses a high-level intent (e.g., "ensure 1ms latency for service X"), which
a controller translates into a specific, pre-defined set of low-level policies and
configurations.
58 This approach is brittle; it cannot adapt to network conditions or service
demands that were not explicitly foreseen by the programmers.
This invention introduces a more advanced, biologically inspired form of IBN, as illustrated in
FIG. 5.
1. Intent Translation into Objectives (Step 510): A high-level business or service intent,
which can be expressed in natural language, is received by the NCOL. Instead of
mapping this to a static policy, a high-level AI model (which can be a conventional LLM
integrated with the NCOL) translates the intent into a set of mathematical objective
functions and constraints that are distributed to the SNNs across the network.
60 For an
intent like "Provide a secure, low-latency channel for a surgical robot swarm," the
objectives might be to minimize latency, maximize the QKD key rate, and keep the packet
drop rate below a certain threshold.
2. Network Self-Organization (Step 520): The distributed SNNs of the NCOL operate as a
collective, with each local SNN agent controlling its local resources (e.g., RAN scheduler,
QKD relay parameters, edge compute allocation). The agents do not follow a central
command. Instead, they continuously adapt their behavior and control parameters,
driven by local plasticity rules (e.g., STDP) that are modulated by the global objective
functions.
61 An agent's actions that contribute positively to the global objectives are
reinforced, strengthening the synaptic pathways that led to those actions. Actions that
are detrimental are weakened.
3. Emergence of Optimal State (Step 530): Through this process of continuous,
distributed, and parallel adaptation, the globally optimal network configuration emerges
from the local interactions of the SNN agents. The network literally learns how to best
fulfill the intent under the current, real-time conditions. If a new, unforeseen type of
interference appears, the network will autonomously learn a new resource allocation
strategy to circumvent it and maintain the intent, without any human intervention or
reprogramming. This aligns with the 6G vision of the network itself becoming a form of
distributed intelligence.
11
Inventive Step 3: Real-Time Predictive Self-Healing
Conventional network maintenance is reactive. An alarm is triggered, and then a remediation
process begins. Predictive maintenance in 5G uses ML to analyze historical data to forecast
potential failures, but this process is often slow and can miss novel, "black swan" failure
modes.
6
The NCOL enables a proactive, real-time self-healing capability that operates at the speed of
the network itself.
1. Multi-Modal Sensing: The NCOL ingests a rich, multi-modal dataset in real-time. This
includes not only network-level data (packet flows, latency, jitter) but also physical-layer
data from ISAC (RF interference patterns, object detection near infrastructure) and
physical sensor data (temperature, vibration, power draw of network components).
7
2. Spatio-Temporal Pattern Recognition: The distributed SNNs are trained to recognize
the subtle, complex spatio-temporal patterns that are precursors to failure. For example,
a specific SNN model might learn that a unique RF interference pattern, combined with a
2-degree increase in a power amplifier's temperature and a 5% increase in a specific
type of packet retransmission, is a high-confidence predictor of a hardware failure within
the next 60 seconds.
64
3. Proactive Remediation: Upon detecting such a precursor pattern, the NCOL triggers an
autonomous self-healing workflow. This can include seamlessly rerouting traffic away
from the at-risk component, provisioning a redundant virtual network function on a
different physical server, and automatically generating a detailed maintenance ticket—all
before any user-perceptible service degradation occurs.
67 This transforms network
maintenance from a reactive, OPEX-intensive process into a proactive, autonomous
function that maximizes service availability and reliability.
IV. Cross-Layer Integration and Operation
The true power of the disclosed architecture lies in the deep, real-time integration between
the three layers, orchestrated by the NCOL. This integration is best exemplified by the
invention's novel approach to network simulation and pre-validation.
Inventive Step 4: The Quantum-State-Aware Digital Twin
Digital Twins are a known concept in network management, representing a virtual replica of
the physical network used for monitoring, simulation, and "what-if" analysis.
69 However,
existing digital twins are purely classical; they can model traffic flows, hardware states, and
software configurations, but they are fundamentally incapable of modeling the probabilistic,
fragile, and non-deterministic nature of a quantum network. An action that appears optimal in
a classical simulation could have disastrous, unforeseen consequences on the quantum state
of the QSL.
This invention introduces a Quantum-State-Aware Digital Twin, a high-fidelity, hybrid
classical-quantum simulation environment managed by the NCOL, as depicted in FIG. 6.
1. Hybrid Modeling: The digital twin 600 comprises two interconnected models. A
classical model 610 represents the 6G Transport Layer 110, simulating everything from RF
propagation to packet queuing and compute resource utilization. A quantum model 620
represents the QSL 120. This is not a full quantum computer simulation, which would be
intractable. Instead, it is a probabilistic simulation that models the quantum state of key
components. For example, a QKD link is not represented as a simple channel but by its
density matrix, which captures its state, including its fidelity, coherence, and
susceptibility to decoherence, informed by real-time data from the physical network.
70
2. Real-Time Synchronization: The twin is not a static model. It is continuously updated
with real-time telemetry from the live network. The classical model 610 is fed with data
from the 6G Transport Layer 110 (e.g., traffic loads, latency measurements). The quantum
model 620 is fed with data from the QSL 120 (e.g., QBER, key rates, entanglement fidelity
measurements). This ensures the twin is always a high-fidelity mirror of the current state
of the entire hybrid network.
3. Neuromorphic-Powered "Future Shots": The NCOL 130 uses this advanced digital twin
for predictive optimization and pre-validation.
69 Before implementing any significant
change in the live network (e.g., rerouting a major traffic trunk, deploying a new network
slice, or changing a QKD path), the NCOL first performs a "future shot" simulation within
the twin. It can simulate the proposed change and project the network's state (both
classical and quantum) forward in time, assessing the full impact of the decision. For
example, it can predict not only that a traffic reroute will reduce latency but also that the
increased data load on a particular fiber will create thermal noise that slightly increases
the QBER on a co-located QKD link, potentially violating a service's security SLA. This
level of cross-layer, predictive insight is impossible with prior art.
This capability enables the network to achieve true self-evolution. The NCOL can continuously
experiment with novel optimization strategies within the safe confines of the digital twin,
validating their efficacy and safety before deploying only the most beneficial changes to the
live network. This creates a perpetual loop of improvement, allowing the network to adapt and
evolve its own operation to meet new challenges and demands over time. The management of
this complex, hybrid simulation is a task for which neuromorphic computing is uniquely suited,
given its ability to handle complex dynamical systems and its potential application in
controlling quantum systems.
